hello i'm andrew lee an omnics logic  fellow and an intern at columbia  university this summer  my project is coding an app in art using  single cell rna-seq fetal brain samples  for the identification of potential  autism risk genes  autism is a neurological condition that  causes limitations in social  communication repetitive and compulsive  actions and also declines in cognitive  ability  there is a high prevalence of autism as  around one percent of total population  has this condition  the similar clinical symptomatology of  neurological disorders also leads to  common misdiagnosis of autism  this is a problem because it serves as a  barrier for the creation of accurate  individualized treatments  and also occupational therapy programs  and learning arrangements depend on  accurate diagnosis  the current methods of diagnosis are the  dsm 5 and genetic testing the dsm-5 is  considered the gold standard diagnostic  criteria used by physicians today  however several studies have indicated  that the dsm-5 is overly broad with its  criteria  as you can see on the right  this is a flowchart of how autism is  diagnosed from the behavioral monitoring  to the genetic testing  and the genetic testing is highly  dependent on discovering new autism-rich  genes  recent studies has estimated that a  thousand or more genes cause autism  however there have only been a hundred  genes are confirmed  a more recent approach is through  machine learning to identify genes that  have similar expression profiles to  already confirmed autism genes  for my project i want to first identify  potential autism risk genes using an  unsupervised machine learning approach  next i want to identify those genes that  have similar expression profiling among  autism-related cell clusters  and this would eliminate some of the  coincidental connections caused by the  unsupervised methods  next i want to improve my research and  the research of others by creating a  publicly available app with a  user-friendly interface that only  requires a single gene name to result in  clear visualization and plots  of more than 20 types of human cells  my data sets for this was an a-risk  asset from chennai 2020  there were two groups of data first was  the human fetal brain sample readings  and the next was the prefrontal cortex  samples  for the identification of possible  autism-related genes i first conducted a  heat map and principal component  analysis  the plots were annotated with around 200  to 200 genes and 121 of those were from  the spark autism database  the app was coded in r using shiny and  shiny dashboard packages and some of the  code was modified from the omnixlogic  biotech transcriptomics course  to continue with the methods i first  used a t-distributed stochastic neighbor  embedding plot the disney plot  and next i also did violin plots to use  single gene inputs to generate  expression levels for more than 20  distinct cell types  and the next was a ridge plot which is  just an alternative to the single gene  to see  the expression levels were then compared  with those of known autism-rich genes  and the genes with similar expression  profiling were determined as possible  autism irish genes  so you can see a schematic of how the  process was conducted first let's use  expression matrix and then the heat map  and principal component analysis with  hierarchical clustering was made  this gave me a list of genes to further  analyze and this i manually inputted on  the app that i created  and this created violin pot disney plots  and ridge plots  and this i compared to the known  autism-rich genes to develop a final  list of candidate autism genes  for the heat map and principle component  analysis with hierarchical clustering  you can see that the risk genes are  clustered near the top with high  functional scores and these are genes  that have already been evaluated as  autism genes  however 10 genes listed were not already  labeled as autism genes and they were  also clustered near the top and you can  see this with the purple and blue  coloring  and this means that they can potentially  serve as candidate autism-rich genes  you can see a similar expression  profiling on the pc-1 of the principal  component analysis  the austen rich genes in blue are  grouped together however you can see  several red dots and these indicate that  these genes are also similarly expressed  these genes from the heat map and  principle component analysis were listed  for the next step in verifying that they  are indeed risk genes  to narrow this down i used a single gene  expression from the app i coded  all the genes from the previous step  were manually inputted one by one into  the coded app and they generated three  plots as you can see from the example  chd 4 which is a known autism gene above  you can see that chd 4 had high  expression in many of the cell clusters  that are linked to autism and also in  terms of the scores where they are on  the tisny plot you can see that is  highly expressed in many cell clusters  and this process was verified for each  of the genes and if they had one  low expression levels or two were not  expressed in autism-related cell types  they were excluded  on their right you can see the cell type  plots and these were the umap and also  the complete gene disney  while they do not help in autism risk  identification you can see that the  cells can be clearly grouped into 26  distinct cell clusters  my research conclusions  is that the app provides automatic plot  rendering and image downloads and this  means that the user can easily visualize  single genes the app is also useful for  other researchers that can want to  correlate genes of interest to  expression levels between cell types  for this study i correlated the  application to autism-risk genes and you  can see that on the heat map and the  principal component analysis there was  apparent clustering of the known autism  genes  and also on the heat map clustering  however there were also some genes that  were  not known as autism risk genes before  but were also grouped with these and  after applying the two selection  criteria five genes were identified as  potential autism-rich genes  these may also be markers of autism on  their right you can see the list of the  five genes identified the discovery of  autism-rich genes can clearly improve  the genetic testing available for autism  which provides more clear and accurate  diagnosis  in the past genetic testing has been  limited by the lack of autism-rich gene  knowledge and the amount of data that's  available for some limitations of my  study  is that brain expression similarities  can often be coincidental and further  functional analysis is needed to verify  that any of the genes are indeed  autism-rich genes  supervised machine learning approaches  and also neural networks can provide  more  autism autism-risk gene discovery and  reduce their risk of false positives  from my future research  this analysis has proved useful in  identifying the five candidate autism  genes but i also want to study their  biological and molecular functions  to extend my project further i want to  continue adding more recent and larger  data sets to use in the app and the  unsupervised gene machine learning  methods i would like to thank mr ellia  brodsky dr harpreet carr and my mentors  at columbia university  thank you for listening
 

